<p>This guide covers the full path: positioning, design handoff, fabric and print choices, manufacturer vetting, MOQ negotiation, sampling, bulk production, quality control, and launch. The supply chain sections run deepest, because that&#39;s where first-time founders burn cash and calendar. Reading in order makes sense if you&#39;re still at the moodboard stage. If your designs are done and you need a production partner, skip to Step 4</p>
<h2 id="why-different">What Makes a Streetwear Brand Different (Before You Spend a Dollar)</h2>
<p>Streetwear is a cultural position, not a product category. A 400gsm hoodie with a bold back print reads as streetwear when it&#39;s tied to a scene (skate, hip hop, graffiti, dance, city commuting, festival circuits) and as a blank with a logo when it isn&#39;t. The commercial consequence is blunt: the same garment costs the factory roughly the same to build regardless of what&#39;s printed on it, while the brand with a real audience charges two to four times the unbranded price</p>
<p>Three structural differences separate a streetwear label from a general fashion brand</p>
<ul class="ar-list">
<li><strong>Drop rhythm replaces the seasonal calendar.</strong> You sell in limited releases of 100 to 500 units, not four-season wholesale books. That reshapes your inventory model, your production batching, and the conversation you have with a factory about repeat runs</li>
<li><strong>Silhouette and weight are the identity.</strong> Oversized fits, boxy bodies, drop shoulders, and heavy fabrics are spec decisions, not accidents. A 180gsm tee and a 240gsm tee look like two different brands on the same body</li>
<li><strong>Community comes before catalog.</strong> People buy the world first. The garment is the entry ticket</li>
</ul>
<p>Four questions worth answering honestly before any money moves:</p>
<ol class="ar-list">
<li>Who specifically wears this? Age band, city, subculture, spending level</li>
<li>Which brands does that buyer already own, and what do you give them that those brands don&#39;t?</li>
<li>Which single product defines the first impression?</li>
<li>Can you describe the brand in one sentence without using the word &quot;premium&quot;?</li>
</ol>
<p>No concrete answer to question 2 means you&#39;re not ready to email a manufacturer. Fabric weight, print method, and trim quality all descend from positioning, and reversing those decisions after bulk fabric is cut costs real money</p>
<h2 id="step-1">Step 1: Define Your Niche, Audience, and Price Position</h2>
<p>Niche selection is a sourcing decision disguised as a marketing one. It determines which fabrics, finishes, and factories you can realistically use. A skate-leaning label lives on 12oz canvas, heavy jersey, and screen print that survives grip tape. Go luxury-street and you&#39;re buying garment dye, custom hardware, and a pattern maker comfortable with multi-panel construction. A sports-inspired line needs moisture-wicking knits and a partner with sublimation capability</p>
<p>Your retail band sets the cost ceiling, and the cost ceiling picks your factory type</p>
<div class="ar-table-wrap"><table class="ar-table">
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Positioning</th>
<th>Typical retail band (tee / hoodie)</th>
<th>Fabric tendency</th>
<th>Production implication</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td>Entry / community-driven</td>
<td>$25–45 / $55–90</td>
<td>180–220gsm jersey, 280–350gsm fleece</td>
<td>Screen print, stock blanks or simple cut-and-sew, small batches viable</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Mid-tier independent</td>
<td>$40–70 / $90–150</td>
<td>220–260gsm jersey, 350–420gsm fleece</td>
<td>Cut-and-sew, puff print and embroidery, custom woven labels</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Premium / luxury-street</td>
<td>$80+ / $180+</td>
<td>260gsm+ jersey, 400gsm+ fleece, garment-dyed</td>
<td>Custom development, hardware, three or more sampling rounds</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>The fastest way to anchor your position: pick three competitors at different price points, buy one garment from each, then take them apart on a table. Count stitches per inch at the side seam. Check whether the neck tape is twill or self-fabric. Wash the printed one five times at 40°C and look at the edges of the graphic. That hour of teardown tells you more about where your product must sit than a week of spreadsheet work</p>
<h2 id="step-2">Step 2: Turn Your Ideas into Tech Packs and Samples</h2>
<p>A tech pack lets a factory quote and build your garment without guessing. A usable one contains:</p>
<ul class="ar-list">
<li>Flat technical sketches: front, back, and detail views</li>
<li>Measurement spec by size with tolerance ranges, typically ±0.5 inch on chest and body length, ±0.25 inch on sleeve and cuff</li>
<li>Fabric spec: composition, GSM, knit type, color reference by Pantone TCX</li>
<li>Construction notes: seam types, stitch density (10 to 12 SPI on jersey is standard), bar tacks at pocket openings, hem and cuff finishing</li>
<li>Print and embroidery placement with dimensions measured from HPS, plus color separations</li>
<li>Label, hangtag, and packaging instructions</li>
<li>Target quantity with the size breakdown</li>
</ul>
<p>Sending a photo and writing &quot;make this&quot; is the most expensive shortcut in apparel. The factory interprets, you correct, and each clarification loop adds two to four days of dead time. In our sampling work with new streetwear brands, the same three gaps surface again and again: rib composition left blank on the collar and cuff, print placement given as &quot;centered&quot; with no measurement down from HPS, and no stated tolerance on body length. Each gap generates a question, each question waits for a time-zone cycle, and a vague tech pack routinely burns two extra confirmation rounds before anyone cuts fabric</p>
<p><strong>Five things to lock before sampling starts:</strong></p>
<ol class="ar-list">
<li><strong>Measurements and tolerances.</strong> Get the spec sheet signed off, including how much variance you&#39;ll accept per point of measure</li>
<li><strong>Fabric and color.</strong> Approve a lab dip or a physical swatch. A Pantone chip on a monitor and a dyed knit almost never match, and dye lots on heavy fleece drift more than on light jersey</li>
<li><strong>Artwork.</strong> Supply vector files at final print size, with separations for multi-color screens</li>
<li><strong>Trims and labels.</strong> Woven labels, printed neck tags, drawcords, custom zipper pulls, and metal eyelets each carry their own minimum and their own lead time, often 10 to 20 days</li>
<li><strong>Sample type and fee.</strong> Clarify whether you&#39;re getting a proto, a fit sample, or a salesman sample, and whether the fee credits back against bulk</li>
</ol>
<h2 id="step-3">Step 3: Choose Fabrics, Weights, and Prints That Fit Streetwear</h2>
<p>Fabric weight is the fastest quality signal in this market. Commodity tees sit at 140 to 160gsm. Streetwear starts at 180gsm and climbs to 240gsm or beyond for statement pieces. Hoodie fleece runs 280 to 450gsm, and 350 to 400gsm hits the balance most buyers want: substantial in the hand, still wearable indoors. Above 450gsm you gain presence and lose sales in warm markets</p>
<p>Print method drives both the look and the cost curve</p>
<div class="ar-table-wrap"><table class="ar-table">
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Method</th>
<th>Effect</th>
<th>Best for</th>
<th>Cost tendency</th>
<th>Notes</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td>Screen print</td>
<td>Flat, bold, durable</td>
<td>Large graphics, multi-color fronts and backs</td>
<td>Low per unit at volume</td>
<td>One setup charge per color; economics work past roughly 300 pieces per design</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Puff print</td>
<td>Raised, tactile, 3D</td>
<td>Logos, chest hits, retro graphics</td>
<td>Medium</td>
<td>Additive cost per hit; wash-test before bulk</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Embroidery</td>
<td>Structured, premium</td>
<td>Chest logos, caps, small marks</td>
<td>Medium to high</td>
<td>Stitch count drives price; backing choice prevents puckering on 180gsm knits</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>DTG</td>
<td>Full-color, photographic</td>
<td>Batches under 100, complex art</td>
<td>High per unit, near-zero setup</td>
<td>Best tool for testing designs before cutting screens</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Garment dye / wash</td>
<td>Vintage, faded, individual</td>
<td>Premium drops, acid and enzyme washes</td>
<td>Medium to high</td>
<td>Batch color variation is built in; write a tolerance into your spec</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Two rules worth following. Match the decoration to the order size: DTG for a 30-piece test, screen print once a single design clears a few hundred units. And wash-test every fabric-plus-print combination before bulk, five cycles at 40°C with a tumble dry. A puff print that cracks on wash three will cost you more in returns and reputation than you saved on unit price</p>
<p>Comparing weights across categories? <a href="/our-services/">Heavyweight cotton and fleece options</a> covers the fabrics and finishes used for hoodies, tees, and sweatpants</p>
<h2 id="step-4">Step 4: How to Find and Vet a Streetwear Manufacturer</h2>
<p>This is where the guide earns its keep. Your manufacturer sets your MOQ, your quality ceiling, your lead times, and how painful year one feels</p>
<h3>Factory vs Trading Company vs Platform</h3>
<div class="ar-table-wrap"><table class="ar-table">
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Supplier type</th>
<th>What they offer</th>
<th>Strengths</th>
<th>Watch-outs</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td>Direct factory</td>
<td>In-house cut-and-sew, usually with named finishing partners</td>
<td>Quality control, direct answers, better pricing as volume grows</td>
<td>Narrower capability range; not every decoration sits in-house</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Trading company / agent</td>
<td>Coordination across multiple factories</td>
<td>Broad capability, one contact, flexible on mixed-category orders</td>
<td>Thinner quality control, markup on unit price, accountability blurs when things go wrong</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>B2B platform</td>
<td>Supplier directory with escrow tools</td>
<td>Easy discovery, payment protection</td>
<td>Verification depth varies widely; the vetting is still your job</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>For a first run under 300 pieces per style, pick a direct factory that accepts small batches. You need fast, accurate sample feedback far more than you need a wide catalog. Once you&#39;re running five or six styles across hoodies, pants, and outerwear, an agent starts to pay for itself</p>
<h3>How to Vet a Supplier</h3>
<p>Verification is a sequence, not a gut call</p>
<ol class="ar-list">
<li><strong>Request a live video call from the production floor.</strong> A photo gallery proves nothing. Ask them to walk the sewing line and the sampling room while you watch</li>
<li><strong>Ask for references in your category.</strong> A shirting factory can sew beautifully and still have no instinct for drop-shoulder blocks or back-print placement</li>
<li><strong>Send a real tech pack as a test inquiry.</strong> Judge the questions they ask back. A factory that flags your missing rib composition before quoting is a factory that will catch problems in bulk</li>
<li><strong>Confirm sampling capability.</strong> In-house pattern makers and a fit sample before bulk, or walk away</li>
<li><strong>Map in-house versus subcontracted.</strong> Printing, embroidery, washing, and dyeing get farmed out constantly. Know whose hands touch your garment</li>
</ol>
<h3>Eight Questions to Ask Before You Commit</h3>
<ul class="ar-list">
<li>What is your MOQ per style and per color, and how does it shift with fabric?</li>
<li>What is your sampling lead time and fee, and does the fee credit against bulk?</li>
<li>Which processes run in-house, and which are subcontracted?</li>
<li>How does your pre-production sample approval process work?</li>
<li>What are your payment terms: deposit percentage and balance timing?</li>
<li>How do you handle defects and short shipments?</li>
<li>What is your production lead time at my quantity, and what pushes it out?</li>
<li>Can you handle my packaging, labeling, and hangtag requirements?</li>
</ul>
