<h2 id="step-5">Step 5: MOQ, Sampling, and Cost Breakdown (What Beginners Get Wrong)</h2>
<h3>Understanding MOQ</h3>
<p>Minimum order quantity looks like a gate. It&#39;s arithmetic. Mills sell by the roll, and a roll of custom-knitted or custom-dyed fleece can yield far more garments than you want to make. Lines also need setup time per style and per color: marker making, cutting, sewing changeovers, screen burning. MOQ is simply the point where that fixed setup spreads thin enough to be worth running</p>
<p>Cut-and-sew streetwear on stock fabric generally starts around 100 to 300 pieces per style and color. Specify custom fabric or custom dyeing and the floor moves to 300 to 500 and up, because the mill&#39;s own minimum takes over. Low-MOQ programs exist and are the right entry point for a first drop, with a higher per-unit price as the trade. Treat that premium as tuition, not waste</p>
<h3>Where Your Money Goes</h3>
<div class="ar-table-wrap"><table class="ar-table">
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Cost stage</th>
<th>What it covers</th>
<th>Commonly underestimated</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td>Design &amp; tech pack</td>
<td>Pattern making, spec development, artwork</td>
<td>Pattern development for original silhouettes</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Sampling</td>
<td>Proto, fit, and PP samples; lab dips</td>
<td>Revision rounds and international courier fees</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Fabric &amp; trims</td>
<td>Shell, rib, lining, labels, hardware</td>
<td>Trim minimums and custom label setup</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Production</td>
<td>Cutting, sewing, finishing, decoration</td>
<td>Screen setup per color, per design</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Packaging</td>
<td>Polybags, hangtags, custom boxes, tissue</td>
<td>Custom box MOQ and freight volume</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Logistics</td>
<td>Freight, duties, customs clearance</td>
<td>Duty rates and port-to-door buffer</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Want a real number for your first drop? <a href="/contact-us/">Request a quote</a> with your tech pack and target quantity. We break out sampling, unit cost, and freight separately so you can see where the money actually sits</p>
<h2 id="step-6">Step 6: Production, Quality Control, and Fulfillment</h2>
<h3>The Pre-Production Sample Is Not Optional</h3>
<p>Before bulk cutting starts, approve a pre-production sample: the exact garment built from final fabric, final trims, and final artwork. The PP sample is your last cheap chance to catch a shade that drifted two steps off the lab dip, a print sitting an inch high, or a body length that crept during grading. Skipping it causes more large-scale rework than any other single decision</p>
<h3>QC Checkpoints</h3>
<p>Three inspection points belong in every order</p>
<ul class="ar-list">
<li><strong>Pre-production:</strong> fabric and trim verified against approved standards, PP sample signed off in writing</li>
<li><strong>In-line:</strong> measurements, stitch quality, and decoration placement checked on the first 50 pieces off the line, not after the last one</li>
<li><strong>Final:</strong> AQL-based random sampling (2.5 for major defects under Level II is the common commercial standard), measurement conformance, packing accuracy, and label correctness before cartons close</li>
</ul>
<p>A workable final checklist covers six things: measurements within tolerance, print and embroidery placement plus adhesion, seam strength and stitch density, color consistency across dye lots, label and hangtag accuracy, and carton condition. Our <a href="/production/">quality control process</a> applies these checkpoints to every bulk order</p>
<h3>Fulfillment and Packaging</h3>
<p>Settle polybag versus custom box, tissue and sticker inserts, size stickers, and flat-fold versus hanging before production opens, because each choice moves your shipping volume and your warehouse handling time. Retrofitting packaging after goods are sewn means repacking labor and a delayed ship date, so put the spec in the PO</p>
<h2 id="step-7">Step 7: Launch, Drop Strategy, and Sales Channels</h2>
<p>The drop model exists to manage inventory risk: produce a limited run, create urgency, sell through, then restock only what demand proved. One hero product, built properly, teaches you more than a scattered six-piece collection that ties up cash in sizes nobody wanted</p>
<p>Channel mix for a new label:</p>
<ul class="ar-list">
<li><strong>Owned storefront</strong> (Shopify or similar): best margin, full control of presentation, and it builds the customer list you&#39;ll need for drop two</li>
<li><strong>Instagram and TikTok:</strong> where discovery actually happens. Content first, product second</li>
<li><strong>Markets, pop-ups, and community events:</strong> cheap physical validation, plus fit feedback you can&#39;t get from a return form</li>
<li><strong>Wholesale:</strong> a later move, once sell-through is proven and your margin can absorb a retailer&#39;s</li>
</ul>
<p>A launch sequence that works: seed 15 to 30 units with people inside the scene, collect fit and quality notes, fix what surfaces, then run the public drop</p>
<h2 id="mistakes">Common Mistakes When Starting a Streetwear Brand</h2>
<ol class="ar-list">
<li><strong>Ordering without a tech pack.</strong> You get mismatched samples and an open-ended revision loop. Build the spec sheet before you contact a single factory</li>
<li>The fit sample arrived looking close enough, so it got approved and the order went straight to bulk. Three hundred hoodies later, every sleeve is an inch short. Round one proves the pattern, round two proves the fit, and the PP sample proves the whole package before any of it multiplies</li>
<li><strong>Picking a factory on price alone.</strong> The cheapest quote usually comes from whoever read your tech pack least carefully. Weigh capability and communication first; negotiate price after</li>
<li><strong>Misjudging lead time.</strong> Sampling, bulk, inspection, and ocean freight stack up fast. Work backward from your drop date, add a buffer, and get every date in writing</li>
<li>How many units of drop one can you afford to still own in six months? That number, not your optimism, sets the order quantity. Dead stock locks up the cash that funds drop two, which is why small first runs plus restocking against real sell-through beats a confident forecast</li>
<li><strong>Working without written terms.</strong> Payment schedule, tolerances, defect remedies, and delay consequences all belong in the PO before the deposit leaves your account</li>
</ol>
