</blockquote>
<h2 id="faq">FAQ: Starting a Streetwear Brand</h2>
<details class="ar-faq-item" open><summary><h3>How much does it cost to start a streetwear brand?</h3></summary><p>Costs move with fabric, quantity, and decoration. A small first run carries sampling fees, fabric and trim costs, production, packaging, and freight, and the per-unit price swings hardest on MOQ and material choice. The practical route is to request a quote with your tech pack and target quantity rather than budgeting from a generic average</p></details>
<details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>What is the minimum order quantity (MOQ) for a streetwear brand?</h3></summary><p>MOQ follows fabric and construction. Stock fabrics usually allow runs of 100 to 300 pieces per style and color, while custom-knit or custom-dyed fabric pushes the floor to 300 to 500 and above because the mill minimum applies. Low-MOQ programs for first drops exist at a higher per-unit price</p></details>
<details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>How long does sampling and production take?</h3></summary><p>Expect one to two weeks per sampling round, and plan for two rounds on a first style. Bulk production then runs roughly three to five weeks depending on quantity and decoration, followed by inspection and freight. Lock every date in writing and keep a buffer between the arrival window and your launch</p></details>
<details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>Do I need a tech pack before contacting a manufacturer?</h3></summary><p>Yes. Sketches, graded measurements, fabric specs, and decoration details are what let a factory quote accurately and sample correctly. Without them you pay for extra clarification rounds in both cash and calendar days</p></details>
<details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>Can I start with a small batch or a single style?</h3></summary><p>Yes, and you should. One hero style in a small batch validates fit, quality, and demand before you commit capital to a wider range. Restock against real sell-through instead of a forecast</p></details>
<details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>How do I find a reliable streetwear manufacturer?</h3></summary><p>Ask for a live production-floor walkthrough, request references in your category, send a test inquiry with a real tech pack, and confirm which processes run in-house. The questions a factory asks you back during that inquiry predict how the relationship will run</p></details>
<details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>Should I use a trading company or a direct factory?</h3></summary><p>For a first small run, a direct factory gives tighter sample feedback and more control. Trading companies earn their margin once you source several categories and need a single coordination point. Either way, verify who physically produces your garment</p></details>
<details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>What fabrics and GSM are best for streetwear?</h3></summary><p>Tees generally sit at 180 to 220gsm jersey, and hoodies at 280 to 450gsm fleece, with 350 to 400gsm the common choice for a heavyweight hoodie that still sells in mild climates. Heavier fabric signals substance, so check mill availability and handle a physical swatch before committing</p></details>
